      "UK websites that handle personal data sit under UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018, and cookies and similar tracking fall under the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations, usually called PECR. The Data (Use and Access) Act 2025 is changing parts of both in stages, including new exemptions for some low-risk analytics cookies, so check current Information Commissioner's Office guidance before relying on any of them. The practical build rule stays the same for now: advertising pixels and non-essential cookies should not load until a visitor actively agrees, and rejecting them should be as easy as accepting. We wire consent in from the start, holding scripts back until consent is given, rather than pasting a banner on top of a site that has already dropped every cookie.",
      "Beyond cookies, we keep data collection deliberate. Contact and quote forms collect only the fields you need, submit over HTTPS to a destination you control, and are covered by a privacy notice that describes what actually happens to the data. Where enquiries flow into a CRM or email platform, we list which third parties receive what, which makes your own records easier to keep. Marketing emails need consent or a genuine existing-customer relationship under PECR, so sign-up forms get clear, unticked consent checkboxes.",
      "<strong>Accessibility.</strong> Public sector bodies have specific accessibility regulations. Private businesses have duties under the Equality Act 2010 not to put disabled people at a substantial disadvantage, and WCAG 2.2 level AA is the practical benchmark most organisations use. We build to it by default: semantic structure, keyboard navigation, visible focus, sufficient colour contrast, labelled forms, captions where there is video and text alternatives for meaningful images. If you also sell to consumers in the EU, the European Accessibility Act may apply to parts of your service there.",
